# Generic credential globs. Deliberately OUTSIDE the audited region below:
# they name no specific file, so there is nothing to verify them against.
*.secret
*.key
*.pem

# === CREDENTIAL RULES: tagged, audited by ktp_secret_scan audit-ignore ===
#
# Every concrete path withheld for secrecy, host-topology or privacy reasons lives
# in this region and carries a tag on the line ABOVE it. `make secrets-audit`
# checks that each tag still says something true, so the list cannot quietly rot
# into a set of rules nobody can justify.
#
#   @secret    file exists and carries credential VALUES -> the scan must hit it
#   @hostinfo  file exists and carries fleet IPs / topology, but no credential
#   @guard     file is ABSENT; the rule exists so it can never be added
#   @privacy   file exists, holds no credential, withheld for a stated reason
#   @local     local stub or runtime artifact; no secrecy claim
#
# ⚠️ A tag must be its own line. In .gitignore syntax `#` only opens a comment at
# the START of a line, so `deploy/.env  # @guard` folds the comment into the
# pattern and the rule silently stops matching anything.
#
# 🔑 A rule matching an ABSENT file is doing its most valuable work, not its least.
# Five rules were commented out on 2026-08-18 for naming files that no longer
# existed; two of those files had been deleted precisely BECAUSE they carried
# secrets. @guard exists so that judgement is never made by hand again.
#
# 🔑 The way to shrink this list is to refactor a file to resolve its secrets at
# run time and then track it -- as monitoring/ktp-server-monitor.py did. Deleting
# lines is not the same thing.

# @guard
# why: relay URL + auth secret. Lives at /etc/ktp/discord-relay.conf on the hosts.
discord-relay.conf

# @hostinfo
deploy/config.yaml

# @guard
# why: canonical secrets filename; regenerating deploy config recreates it.
deploy/.env

# @local
# why: stub with empty values; the tracked discord.ini.example is the template.
config/online/discord.ini

# @hostinfo
config/online/hltv_recorder.ini

# @guard
# why: the live fleet config carries rcon_password and sv_password. Pulling one
# off a host to diff is routine, which is exactly when this rule earns its keep.
config/online/dodserver.cfg

# @guard
# why: retired 2026-08-17; embedded a live Discord webhook and was neither tracked
# nor ignored, so a `git add` from a data-server checkout would have published it.
# Superseded by ktp-systemd-alert.py. Correct end state is deleted + webhook revoked.
scripts/discord-alert.sh

# @hostinfo
scripts/ktp-scheduled-restart.sh

# @local
# why: locally edited copy; scripts/ktp-organize-hltv-demos.sh.example is tracked
# and is the source of truth. Candidate to un-ignore -- it carries neither a
# credential nor a host address.
scripts/ktp-organize-hltv-demos.sh

# @secret
scripts/hltv-api.py

# @secret
scripts/ktp-backup.sh

# @hostinfo
scripts/audit-fleet.json

# @hostinfo
scripts/audit_fleet_versions.py

# @hostinfo
scripts/verify_new_staged.py

# @secret
scripts/deploy-chrt-to-servers.py

# @hostinfo
scripts/profiling-report.py

# @hostinfo
provision/clone-ktp-stack.sh

# @hostinfo
ktp_gameserver_setup.md

# @secret
ktp_dataserver_setup.md

# @secret
infrastructure.md

# @privacy
# why: embeds a suggested account password and remote-access setup for a machine
# handed to an owner. Share privately, never publish.
provision/LAN-PC-OWNER-SETUP.md

# @privacy
# why: rendered form of LAN-PC-OWNER-SETUP.md, same content.
provision/LAN-PC-OWNER-SETUP.html

# @privacy
# why: rendered form of LAN-PC-OWNER-SETUP.md, same content.
provision/LAN-PC-OWNER-SETUP.pdf

# @hostinfo
COSTS.md

# @privacy
# why: maps every player's SteamID to every alias they have used. Not a
# credential, but publishing it permanently deanonymizes alt accounts.
docs/ktpr_mcp/roster.csv

# === END CREDENTIAL RULES ===

# monitoring/ktp-server-monitor.py — un-ignored: it now resolves its rcon password
# at run time and embeds no secret, which is the refactor the policy above asks for.

